#align(center)[#text(size: 16pt, weight: "bold")[The free uniform spanning forest is a factor of IID]]
#align(center)[OpenAI]
#align(center)[September 25, 2026]

== 1 Introduction <sec:introduction>

Uniform spanning forests are canonical probability measures on the edges of an infinite graph. Pemantle constructed infinite-volume spanning-tree limits on the integer lattice #cite(label("Pemantle1991")) Theorem 2.3; Benjamini, Lyons, Peres and Schramm developed their general graph theory #cite(label("BLPS2001")). The free uniform spanning forest is obtained by taking weak limits of uniform spanning trees on finite connected exhaustions, without identifying boundary vertices. The limit exists and is independent of the exhaustion. We recall its classical construction from finite tree determinants in Proposition 6; see also Benjamini, Lyons, Peres, and Schramm #cite(label("BLPS2001")) Section 5. A factor of IID description asks whether this global random object can be constructed, equivariantly and measurably, from independent labels attached to the vertices. Lyons explicitly discussed the FUSF factor question for the group action on Cayley graphs in his report for the 2013 Oberwolfach workshop #cite(label("Lyons2013")) p. 2392.

On transient graphs, the wired forest has an IID construction through cycle popping and Wilson’s algorithm rooted at infinity; see #cite(label("BLPS2001")) Theorem 5.1. Angel, Ray and Spinka give an explicit graph-factor formulation, retaining the transient graph scope and crediting the earlier construction #cite(label("AngelRaySpinka2024")) Section 4. Determinantal structure also provides factor constructions in amenable group settings: Lyons and Thom prove Bernoulli isomorphism for equivariant determinantal measures on amenable Cayley graphs, with respect to the group action #cite(label("LyonsThom2016")) Corollary 7.4. This Bernoulli-isomorphism result concerns the specified group action; it does not by itself provide one rule on arbitrary graphs equivariant under every graph isomorphism.

For general graph laws, Timár proves FIID representability of the free forest on recurrent unimodular random graphs and, more generally, on invariantly amenable unimodular random graphs #cite(label("Timar2025")) arXiv version, Theorem 1 and Corollary 5. Under invariant amenability he obtains the stronger conclusion of a finite-valued finitary factor #cite(label("Timar2025")) arXiv version, Theorem 2(1). His construction uses compatible monotone finite-volume limits along a hyperfinite exhaustion. The introduction to that December 2025 version states the unrestricted FUSF factor question as open. We prove the unrestricted graphwise statement by a different construction: the same Borel rule works for every graph in our class, independently of any random-graph law. The challenge is to obtain a single equivariant sample from the infinite-volume law while controlling all edges jointly.

=== 1.1 Statement <statement>

The graphs in our main result are infinite, connected, locally finite, simple, and undirected. Write $upright("FUSF")_G$ for the free uniform spanning forest law on ${ 0 \, 1 }^(E \( G \))$. Graphs with a distinguished undirected edge and vertex labels carry the usual Borel structure generated by finite marked neighborhoods. A rule is #emph[root independent] if it uses no distinguished vertex. Equivariance means invariance under every isomorphism of labeled graphs carrying the distinguished edge to the distinguished edge.

#thm([Theorem 1.1.])[
  #emph[There is one Borel, root-independent, equivariant rule 
$ Phi \( G \, U \, e \) in { 0 \, 1 } $

 such that, for every infinite connected locally finite simple undirected unweighted graph $G$ and independent $"Uniform" \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ vertex labels $U = \( U_v \)_(v in V \( G \))$, the entire edge set 
$ { e in E \( G \) : Phi \( G \, U \, e \) = 1 } $

 has law $upright("FUSF")_G$.]
] <thm:main>

#thm([Corollary 1.2.])[
  #emph[The free uniform spanning forest is a graph factor of IID under every unimodular law supported on the stated class of graphs. The same rule works for every such law.]
] <cor:unimodular>

Here unimodularity means the mass-transport identity

$ bb(E) sum_(v in V \( G \)) f \( G \, o \, v \) = bb(E) sum_(v in V \( G \)) f \( G \, v \, o \) $

for every nonnegative Borel function of a graph with two distinguished vertices. Theorem 1 is a graphwise statement and does not use this identity. In particular, no amenability, transience, uniform degree bound, or degree moment is assumed. The result asserts an ordinary Borel factor, without a finitary coding radius or a finite input alphabet.

=== 1.2 Strongly Rayleigh processes <strongly-rayleigh-processes>

The sampling criterion also applies to a broader class of binary laws. For a finite set $F$, a probability measure $mu$ on ${ 0 \, 1 }^F$ is #emph[strongly Rayleigh] if its generating polynomial

$ g_mu \( z \) = sum_(x in { 0 \, 1 }^F) mu \( x \) product_(i in F) z_i^(x_i) $

is nonzero whenever $"Im" z_i > 0$ for every $i in F$. For a countable index set, we require every finite-coordinate marginal to be strongly Rayleigh, following Borcea, Brändén, and Liggett #cite(label("BorceaBrandenLiggett2009")) Definition 2.13.

For a countable group $Gamma$, write $tau_g$ for the regular left action on coordinate families, given by $\( tau_g a \)_h = a_(g^(- 1) h)$.

#thm([Theorem 1.3.])[
  (Strongly Rayleigh processes on countable groups).  #emph[Let $Gamma$ be a countable group and let $nu$ be a strongly Rayleigh probability law on ${ 0 \, 1 }^Gamma$ invariant under every $tau_g$. There is a Borel map 
$ Phi_nu : \[ 0 \, 1 \]^Gamma arrow.r { 0 \, 1 }^Gamma $

 such that $Phi_nu \( tau_g u \) = tau_g Phi_nu \( u \)$ for every $g in Gamma$ and every $u in \[ 0 \, 1 \]^Gamma$, and such that $Phi_nu \( U \)$ has law $nu$ when the coordinates of $U$ are independent $"Uniform" \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ variables.]
] <thm:sr-group>

For a self-adjoint positive contraction $K$ on $ell^2 \( Gamma \)$, the determinantal law of a random subset $X subset.eq Gamma$ is characterized by the finite inclusion probabilities

$ bb(P) \( A subset.eq X \) = det \( K_(g \, h) \)_(g \, h in A) #h(2em) upright("for every finite ") A subset.eq Gamma . $

#thm([Corollary 1.4.])[
  (Determinantal processes on countable groups).  #emph[Let $Gamma$ be a countable group and let $K$ be a self-adjoint positive contraction on $ell^2 \( Gamma \)$, so $0 lt.eq K lt.eq I$. If the determinantal probability law with kernel $K$ is invariant under the regular left action, then it is a factor of IID under that action. In particular, this holds whenever $K$ commutes with the left regular representation of $Gamma$.]
] <cor:dpp-group>

These statements concern the regular action on the group itself, not an arbitrary action on another index set. The factor may depend on $Gamma$ and $nu$. No amenability or finite-generation assumption is imposed, and finite groups are allowed. The conclusion is a Borel factor of IID; it does not assert a finitary coding or a Bernoulli isomorphism. In particular, it is distinct from the amenable-group Bernoulli-isomorphism result of Lyons and Thom cited above. Their broader determinantal factor question appears in #cite(label("LyonsThom2016")) Question 7.7; Corollary 4 treats the regular action on the group itself.

=== 1.3 The construction <the-construction>

Let $X$ have the target forest law and observe its edge indicators through independent Brownian noises:

$ Y_e \( t \) = t X_e + B_e \( t \) . $

For finitely many observed edges, the posterior is an exponential tilt of the forest law. The main quantitative input is the following response identity for a finite weighted spanning tree. If $p_e$ is the probability of including edge $e$ and $h_f$ is the logarithm of the weight of edge $f$, then

$ sum_f lr(|frac(partial p_e, partial h_f)|) = 2 p_e \( 1 - p_e \) lt.eq 1 / 2 . $

Negative off-diagonal covariances and the fixed size of a finite tree give this identity. Passing it through finite-volume limits yields an everywhere-defined posterior drift $b \( t \, y \)$ whose response to bounded changes of the field is uniformly Lipschitz, even when the fields themselves are unbounded across the graph.

Subtracting the posterior drift gives the innovations

$ hat(W)_e \( t \) = Y_e \( t \) - integral_0^t b_e \( s \, Y \( s \) \) thin d s . $

They are independent Brownian motions. The response bound makes the integral equation

$ Z_e \( t \) = w_e \( t \) + integral_0^t b_e \( s \, Z \( s \) \) thin d s $

uniquely solvable by a Borel Picard iteration for every continuous input field $w$. Only differences between iterates are bounded; there is no assumption that a countable Brownian field takes values in $ell^oo$. Thus $Y = Z \( hat(W) \)$, and independent Brownian inputs to this deterministic decoder reproduce the observation law. Integer-time slopes recover $X$.

Proposition 12 isolates the resulting sampling criterion for countable binary laws with uniformly controlled finite-tilt responses. The criterion separates the probabilistic observation argument from the quantitative property of the target measure. For spanning forests, intrinsic edge-centered balls make the decoder equivariant across graphs. Finally, independent vertex labels supply the independent edge paths by a local allocation of disjoint random coordinates.

This construction adapts an established observation mechanism. Stochastic localization was introduced by Eldan #cite(label("Eldan2013")); a finite-dimensional form with fixed noise covariance appears in #cite(label("Eldan2020")) Section 2, Proposition 7. El Alaoui and Montanari #cite(label("ElAlaouiMontanari2022")) Section 3, Theorem 2 develop its Gaussian-channel observation and innovation formulation. A particularly close antecedent is Nam, Sly and Zhang #cite(label("NamSlyZhang2022")) Theorem 1 and Section 2.2: for free Ising measures on regular trees in their parameter range, they obtain FIID codings through Brownian-driven systems with posterior-mean drift and recover spins at large times. Here we prove the countable-coordinate posterior and joint innovation identities, then use the uniform response bound to obtain an inverse defined at every continuous input.

A companion #cite(label("SharpFreeIsing2026")) Theorem 1.1 gives the sharp FIID threshold for the free zero-field ferromagnetic Ising law on regular trees, including the critical case, with a factor that can be chosen to commute with every tree automorphism on every label input. Its conditional-copy recovery differs from our uniform-response inverse.

For strongly Rayleigh laws, symmetric homogenization supplies a fixed-size strongly Rayleigh extension, and the Rayleigh inequality gives nonpositive off-diagonal covariances #cite(label("BorceaBrandenLiggett2009")) Definition 2.12 and Theorems 4.1–4.2. The covariance-row bound then holds with the same constant $1 \/ 2$, as an inequality rather than the weighted-tree identity. Translates of one finite exhaustion of $Gamma$ make the resulting drift equivariant for the regular group action.

Section 2 reconstructs the free limit and establishes the response estimate and the canonical drift. Section 3 proves the posterior and innovation identities. The deterministic inverse and sampling criterion follow in Section 4. Section 5 constructs the single Borel graph rule and proves Theorem 1. Section 6 proves Theorem 3 and Corollary 4.

== 2 Weighted trees and a canonical response field <sec:response>

The analytic input is a response estimate whose constant is independent of the number of edges and of their weights. We first prove it on finite graphs, then use the free spanning-tree limit to construct a drift on every field of real edge marks. The finite determinant and transfer-current identities are classical; see Burton and Pemantle #cite(label("BurtonPemantle1993")) and the weighted formulation in #cite(label("BLPS2001")) Section 4. We prove the needed identities and their free-limit consequence below.

#thm([Lemma 2.1.])[
  (Weighted-tree response).  #emph[Let $H$ be a finite connected graph with at least two vertices, and let $cal(T) \( H \)$ denote its set of spanning trees. For $h in bb(R)^(E \( H \))$, give each $T in cal(T) \( H \)$ probability proportional to $exp \( sum_(f in T) h_f \)$, and write $xi_e = upright(bold(1))_({ e in T })$ and $p_e \( h \) = bb(E)_h xi_e$. For every $e in E \( H \)$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.1)")
 $ frac(partial p_e, partial h_f) = "Cov"_h \( xi_e \, xi_f \) \, #h(2em) sum_(f in E \( H \)) lr(|frac(partial p_e, partial h_f)|) = 2 p_e \( h \) \( 1 - p_e \( h \) \) lt.eq 1 / 2 . $ <eq:covariance-row>]

 In particular, for all $h \, h' in bb(R)^(E \( H \))$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.2)")
 $ \| p_e \( h \) - p_e \( h' \) \| lt.eq 1 / 2 max_(f in E \( H \)) \| h_f - h'_f \| . $ <eq:tree-response>]

]
] <lem:tree-response>

#proof[
  Set $c_f = e^(h_f)$. Orient the edges temporarily, delete one row of the oriented incidence matrix, and denote the resulting columns by $a_f$. The reduced weighted Laplacian is
  $ L = sum_(f in E \( H \)) c_f a_f a_f^(sans(T)) . $
  Write $r = \| V \( H \) \| - 1$. Cauchy–Binet, applied to the reduced incidence matrix with column $f$ multiplied by $sqrt(c_f)$, gives
  $ det L = sum_(F subset.eq E \( H \)\
\| F \| = r) det \( a_f : f in F \)^2 product_(f in F) c_f = sum_(T in cal(T) \( H \)) product_(f in T) c_f = : cal(Z)_H \( h \) . $
  For the second equality, a set containing a cycle has dependent incidence columns, while an acyclic set of $r$ edges is a spanning tree. Its reduced incidence determinant has absolute value one: expand at a leaf other than the vertex whose row was deleted and continue by induction. Connectedness supplies a spanning tree, so $cal(Z)_H \( h \) > 0$ and $L$ is invertible.
  Differentiating the finite partition sum gives both $p_e = partial_(h_e) log cal(Z)_H$ and the covariance identity in (2.1). Differentiating the determinant instead gives
  $ p_e = c_e a_e^(sans(T)) L^(- 1) a_e . $
  Since $partial_(h_f) L^(- 1) = - c_f L^(- 1) a_f a_f^(sans(T)) L^(- 1)$, for distinct edges $e \, f$ we obtain
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.3)")
   $ "Cov"_h \( xi_e \, xi_f \) = - c_e c_f #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] a_e^(sans(T)) L^(- 1) a_f #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]^2 lt.eq 0 . $ <eq:negative-square>]
  Every spanning tree has exactly $r$ edges. Consequently
  $ sum_(f in E \( H \)) "Cov"_h \( xi_e \, xi_f \) = "Cov"_h #h(-0.16667em) (xi_e \, sum_(f in E \( H \)) xi_f) = 0 . $
  The diagonal covariance is $p_e \( 1 - p_e \)$; all the other covariances are nonpositive by (2.3). Their absolute sum is therefore $2 p_e \( 1 - p_e \) lt.eq 1 \/ 2$, as claimed. This also covers a bridge: its inclusion probability is one and its entire covariance row is zero. Finally, integrate the gradient along the segment from $h$ to $h'$ to obtain (2.2). The estimate holds at every point of that segment, without a restriction on the weights. ◻
]

=== 2.1 The free limit from finite determinants <the-free-limit-from-finite-determinants>

We include the free-limit construction, so that the passage from finite weighted trees to the infinite law has an explicit foundation. The relevant space is the closure of the finite cycle flows; no description of all square-summable divergence-free flows on the infinite graph is needed. This cycle-space description is classical; see #cite(label("BLPS2001")) Proposition 7.1 and Theorem 7.8.

#thm([Proposition 2.2.])[
  (Free exhaustion limit).  #emph[Let $G$ be an infinite connected locally finite simple graph. If $H_1 subset.eq H_2 subset.eq dots.h.c$ are finite connected subgraphs with $union.big_n E \( H_n \) = E \( G \)$, the uniform spanning-tree laws on $H_n$, extended by absent edges outside $H_n$, converge weakly on ${ 0 \, 1 }^(E \( G \))$. Their limit is independent of the exhaustion, is supported on spanning forests, and is transported by every graph isomorphism. We denote this law by $upright("FUSF")_G$.]
] <prop:free-limit>

#proof[
  Temporarily orient all edges of $G$. For a finite connected subgraph $H$ with at least two vertices, let $A_H$ be its reduced incidence matrix and put
  $ K_H = A_H^(sans(T)) \( A_H A_H^(sans(T)) \)^(- 1) A_H . $
  For each finite $F subset.eq E \( H \)$, the matrix-tree calculation in Lemma 5, followed by the rectangular determinant identity, gives the polynomial identity
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.4)")
   $ bb(E) product_(e in F) \( 1 + z_e xi_e \) & = frac(det \( A_H A_H^(sans(T)) + A_F "diag" \( z \) A_F^(sans(T)) \), det \( A_H A_H^(sans(T)) \))\
 & = det #h(-0.16667em) (I_F + "diag" \( z \) \( K_H \)_(F \, F)) . $ <eq:finite-inclusion-polynomial>]
  Here $A_F$ consists of the columns indexed by $F$. The first equality can first be read for $z_e > - 1$ as a change of edge weights, and then holds for all $z$ because both sides are polynomials. The identity $det \( I + U V \) = det \( I + V U \)$ used in the second line follows by eliminating blocks in $mat(delim: "(", I, U; - V, I)$ in either order. Expanding the last determinant in its diagonal variables and comparing coefficients yields
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.5)")
   $ bb(P) \( F subset.eq T_H \) = det \( K_H \)_(F \, F) . $ <eq:finite-inclusion-minor>]
  This includes sets larger than $\| V \( H \) \| - 1$: the corresponding minor and tree-inclusion probability are both zero. No minor is inverted.
  Regard finite edge vectors as vectors of $ell^2 \( E \( G \) \)$ by zero extension. Let $C_H = ker A_H$ in the coordinates of $E \( H \)$, and let $Q_H$ be the orthogonal projection onto those coordinates. The matrix $K_H$ is the orthogonal projection onto the row space of $A_H$, so its zero extension is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.6)")
   $ K_H = Q_H - P_(C_H) . $ <eq:finite-cycle-projection>]
  Deleting a different incidence row does not change $C_H$: the full incidence matrix has entries summing to zero in each column, so zero divergence at all but one vertex forces zero divergence at the remaining vertex. The space $C_H$ is exactly the span of its oriented cycle flows. Indeed, choose a spanning tree of $H$ and subtract the fundamental cycle flow for each non-tree edge to eliminate that edge’s coefficient. The remaining circulation is supported on a tree and vanishes by successive leaf removal.
  Write $C_n = C_(H_n)$. These spaces increase under zero extension: a circulation supported on $H_n$ still has zero divergence at every vertex of $H_(n + 1)$. Every finite cycle of $G$ is eventually contained in $H_n$, because the exhaustion includes every edge. Therefore the closure
  $ C = overline(union.big_n C_n) $
  is the closed span of all finite cycle flows of $G$, independent of the chosen exhaustion, whether or not its members are induced subgraphs. The projections $P_(C_n)$ converge strongly to $P_C$. To see this directly, for $m gt.eq n$ and $x in ell^2 \( E \( G \) \)$ orthogonality gives
  $ parallel P_(C_m) x - P_(C_n) x parallel^2 = parallel P_(C_m) x parallel^2 - parallel P_(C_n) x parallel^2 . $
  The squared norms increase and are bounded by $parallel x parallel^2$, so the projected vectors are Cauchy. Their limit lies in $C$, and its difference from $x$ is orthogonal to every $C_n$, hence to $C$. This identifies the limit as $P_C x$. Also $Q_(H_n) arrow.r I$ strongly. Thus the zero-extended $K_(H_n)$ converge strongly to $K = I - P_C$, and every fixed finite principal minor converges to that of $K$.
  For disjoint finite edge sets $A \, B$, inclusion–exclusion now gives a limit for
  $ bb(P) \( A subset.eq T_(H_n) \, med B ∩ T_(H_n) = diameter \) = sum_(J subset.eq B) \( - 1 \)^(\| J \|) det \( K_(H_n) \)_(A union J \, A union J) $
  once $A union B subset.eq E \( H_n \)$; the empty determinant is one. These limits are nonnegative, normalized and consistent on every finite cube, since they are limits of actual probabilities. To construct their countable law explicitly, temporarily enumerate the edges and recursively split $\[ 0 \, 1 \)$ into half-open intervals of the prescribed binary-prefix masses. A uniform point selects one successive prefix at every depth and hence a configuration with these finite marginals. Zero-mass intervals may be omitted. Cylinder probabilities uniquely determine the resulting law, so the temporary enumeration affects neither the law nor the graph rule constructed later. Continuous functions on the compact countable binary product are uniformly approximable by finite-coordinate functions; cylinder convergence consequently proves weak convergence.
  For every finite cycle, the event that all its edges are present has probability zero in every sufficiently late tree law and hence in the limit. There are only countably many finite cycles, so the limit is acyclic on the full vertex set. Reversing any orientations conjugates each finite principal matrix by a diagonal matrix with entries $plus.minus 1$ and preserves its determinant. Graph isomorphisms transport the finite cycle space and all the limiting cylinder probabilities. This proves the claimed independence and invariance. ◻
]

=== 2.2 Finite tilts and the canonical drift <finite-tilts-and-the-canonical-drift>

Fix now an infinite connected locally finite simple undirected graph $G$. Its edge set $E = E \( G \)$ is countable. Write $nu = upright("FUSF")_G$ for the free uniform spanning forest law, viewed as a law on ${ 0 \, 1 }^E$. For $e in E$, finite $S subset.eq E$, $t gt.eq 0$, and $y in bb(R)^E$, define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.7)")
 $ b_(e \, S) \( t \, y \) = frac(integral x_e exp #h(-0.16667em) (sum_(f in S) \( y_f - t \/ 2 \) x_f) thin d nu \( x \), integral exp #h(-0.16667em) (sum_(f in S) \( y_f - t \/ 2 \) x_f) thin d nu \( x \)) . $ <eq:finite-tilt>]

The denominator is positive, since its integrand is at least $exp \( sum_(f in S) min { y_f - t \/ 2 \, 0 } \)$.

#thm([Lemma 2.3.])[
  (Finite-field forest response).  #emph[The function $b_(e \, S)$ takes values in $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$ and is continuous in $\( t \, \( y_f \)_(f in S) \)$. For all $t gt.eq 0$ and $y \, y' in bb(R)^E$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.8)")
 $ \| b_(e \, S) \( t \, y \) - b_(e \, S) \( t \, y' \) \| lt.eq 1 / 2 max_(f in S) \| y_f - y'_f \| \, $ <eq:finite-response>]

 where the maximum over the empty set is defined to be zero.]
] <lem:forest-response>

#proof[
  Both integrals in (2.7) are finite sums over the possible configurations on $S union { e }$. Positivity of the denominator proves continuity, and the range follows from $0 lt.eq x_e lt.eq 1$. If $S$ is empty, the function is the constant $nu \( x_e = 1 \)$.
  For nonempty $S$, take a finite connected free exhaustion $H_m$ of $G$. For all sufficiently large $m$, it contains $S union { e }$. Give its edges log weights $y_f - t \/ 2$ for $f in S$ and zero elsewhere. The resulting tree inclusion probability is the ratio in (2.7) with the uniform spanning-tree law on $H_m$ in place of $nu$. Lemma 5 bounds the difference between this probability and the one using $y'$ by the right side of (2.8). With $S \, t \, y$ fixed, both integrands are bounded cylinder functions, so their expectations converge under the free spanning-tree limit. The limiting denominator is strictly positive by the displayed lower bound. Thus the ratios converge to $b_(e \, S) \( t \, y \)$, and likewise for $y'$. Passing to the limit proves (2.8). ◻
]

To specify one drift at every field, we now fix the outer exhaustion intrinsically. If $e = { u \, v }$, let $H_n \( e \)$ be the induced subgraph on

$ { z in V \( G \) : min { d_G \( z \, u \) \, d_G \( z \, v \) } lt.eq n } \, #h(2em) n gt.eq 1 \, $

and set $S_n \( e \) = E \( H_n \( e \) \)$. These graphs are finite and connected, increase with $n$, and exhaust $G$. Define, for every $t gt.eq 0$ and every $y in bb(R)^E$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.9)")
 $ b_e \( t \, y \) = limsup_(n arrow.r oo) b_(e \, S_n \( e \)) \( t \, y \) \, #h(2em) b \( t \, y \) = \( b_e \( t \, y \) \)_(e in E) . $ <eq:drift>]

#thm([Proposition 2.4.])[
  (Canonical drift).  #emph[Each $b_e$ is a jointly Borel function on $\[ 0 \, oo \) times bb(R)^E$ with values in $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$, where $bb(R)^E$ has its product Borel structure. The construction commutes with graph isomorphisms and does not select a root. For every $t gt.eq 0$ and $y \, y' in bb(R)^E$ such that $sup_(f in E) \| y_f - y'_f \| < oo$, it satisfies 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.10)")
 $ sup_(e in E) \| b_e \( t \, y \) - b_e \( t \, y' \) \| lt.eq 1 / 2 sup_(f in E) \| y_f - y'_f \| . $ <eq:response-bound>]

 Neither $y$ nor $y'$ is required to have bounded coordinates.]
] <prop:canonical-drift>

#proof[
  The range and Borel assertions follow from Lemma 7 and the countable limsup in (2.9). If $d = sup_f \| y_f - y'_f \| < oo$, that lemma gives
  $ b_(e \, S_n \( e \)) \( t \, y \) lt.eq b_(e \, S_n \( e \)) \( t \, y' \) + d \/ 2 $
  for every $e \, n$. Taking limsups and then interchanging $y \, y'$ proves (2.10).
  For the intrinsic nature of the construction, the inner limit has the explicit expression
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.11)")
   $ b_(e \, S_n \( e \)) \( t \, y \) = lim_(m arrow.r oo \, med m gt.eq n) frac(sum_(T in cal(T) \( H_m \( e \) \)) upright(bold(1))_({ e in T }) exp #h(-0.16667em) (sum_(f in T ∩ S_n \( e \)) \( y_f - t \/ 2 \)), sum_(T in cal(T) \( H_m \( e \) \)) exp #h(-0.16667em) (sum_(f in T ∩ S_n \( e \)) \( y_f - t \/ 2 \))) . $ <eq:canonical-finite-formula>]
  This is exactly the fixed-finite-field limit used in the proof of Lemma 7. Each finite ratio is invariant under isomorphisms preserving the distinguished undirected edge and its field marks. The prescribed limits preserve that property. The same finite formulas therefore specify the drift as the graph varies; their use in a Borel graph rule will be made explicit below. ◻
]

The order of the two spatial limits matters: in (2.11), $n$ is fixed while $m$ tends to infinity, and only afterwards is the limsup in $n$ taken. We neither differentiate an infinite-volume partition function nor use a cardinality identity for an infinite forest. We also do not require the outer limit to exist at arbitrary fields. The limsup supplies a bounded Borel version everywhere, and (2.10) controls its change under every bounded perturbation of a possibly unbounded field.

== 3 Brownian observations and their innovations <sec:observation>

Subtracting the posterior mean drift from the observations will produce a family of independent Brownian paths. Section 4 will use a uniform response bound to reconstruct the observations deterministically from this family.

The finite-dimensional innovation identity is classical in nonlinear filtering; see Fujisaki, Kallianpur, and Kunita #cite(label("FujisakiKallianpurKunita1972")) Lemma 2.2. Here the probabilistic argument applies to any probability law $nu$ on ${ 0 \, 1 }^I$, where $I$ is countable. For each $i in I$, fix finite sets $S_n \( i \)$ increasing to $I$. Define $b_(i \, S)$ by the finite-tilt formula (2.7), with this law $nu$, and define $b_i$ by (2.9). These functions are bounded and jointly Borel. No response bound is needed in this section.

On a reference probability space let $X tilde.op nu$, and independently let $\( B_i \)_(i in I)$ be independent standard Brownian motions. Set

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.1)")
 $ Y_i \( t \) = t X_i + B_i \( t \) \, #h(2em) cal(H)_t = sigma { Y_i \( s \) : i in I \, med 0 lt.eq s lt.eq t } . $ <eq:observation>]

We use these raw observation sigma-fields; no completion or right-continuity assumption on the filtration is necessary.

#thm([Lemma 3.1.])[
  (Posterior drift).  #emph[For every fixed $t gt.eq 0$ and $i in I$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.2)")
 $ b_i \( t \, Y \( t \) \) = bb(E) \[ X_i divides cal(H)_t \] #h(2em) upright("almost surely") . $ <eq:posterior>]

]
] <lem:posterior>

#proof[
  For finite $S subset I$, write $cal(H)_t^S = sigma { Y_j \( s \) : j in S \, med 0 lt.eq s lt.eq t }$. When $t > 0$, the density of the endpoint vector $\( Y_j \( t \) \)_(j in S)$, conditional on $X$, relative to independent $N \( 0 \, t \)$ variables is
  $ exp #h(-0.16667em) (sum_(j in S) \( y_j - t \/ 2 \) X_j) . $
  Here $X_j^2 = X_j$. Moreover,
  $ Y_j \( s \) - s / t Y_j \( t \) = B_j \( s \) - s / t B_j \( t \) \, #h(2em) 0 lt.eq s lt.eq t . $
  These bridge paths are jointly independent of $X$ and the endpoint vector. Indeed, at finitely many times this follows from zero Gaussian covariances and independence of $B$ from $X$; continuity and rational-time evaluation extend it to the paths. Bayes’ formula therefore gives
  $ b_(i \, S) \( t \, Y \( t \) \) = bb(E) \[ X_i divides cal(H)_t^S \] #h(2em) upright("almost surely") . $
  At $t = 0$ both sides equal $bb(E) X_i$.
  For fixed $i \, t$, the sigma-fields $cal(H)_t^(S_n \( i \))$ increase to generate $cal(H)_t$. The bounded conditional expectations $M_n = bb(E) \[ X_i divides cal(H)_t^(S_n \( i \)) \]$ converge almost surely to $bb(E) \[ X_i divides cal(H)_t \]$. We recall a short verification of this last step. For $m gt.eq n$,
  $ bb(E) \| M_m - M_n \|^2 = bb(E) M_m^2 - bb(E) M_n^2 . $
  Choose $n_k arrow.t oo$ so that this difference is at most $2^(- 4 k)$ whenever $m gt.eq n = n_k$. The maximal inequality obtained by conditioning on the first crossing gives
  $ bb(P) #h(-0.16667em) (max_(n_k lt.eq r lt.eq m) \| M_r - M_(n_k) \| > 2^(- k)) lt.eq 2^(2 k) bb(E) \| M_m - M_(n_k) \|^2 lt.eq 2^(- 2 k) . $
  To see the first inequality, split into the disjoint events of a first crossing at $r$ and apply conditional Jensen to $M_m - M_(n_k)$ at that stage. Letting $m arrow.r oo$ and summing over $k$ proves that the full sequence is almost surely Cauchy. Its bounded limit has the same integral as $X_i$ against every event in each finite-stage sigma-field. A monotone-class argument identifies it with $bb(E) \[ X_i divides cal(H)_t \]$. Thus the limsup defining $b_i$ is an actual limit along the observations at this fixed time, and (3.2) follows. ◻
]

Define the #emph[innovations] by ordinary scalar time integrals:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.3)")
 $ hat(W)_i \( t \) = Y_i \( t \) - integral_0^t b_i \( s \, Y \( s \) \) thin d s . $ <eq:innovation>]

#thm([Lemma 3.2.])[
  (Joint innovation law).  #emph[The family $\( hat(W)_i \)_(i in I)$ has the law of independent standard Brownian motions. Its finite-coordinate increments after time $u$ are independent of $cal(H)_u$.]
] <lem:innovation>

#proof[
  Fix $T < oo$. The map $\( s \, omega \) mapsto Y \( s \, omega \)$ on $\[ 0 \, T \]$ is measurable for $cal(B) \( \[ 0 \, T \] \) ⊗ cal(H)_T$, coordinate by coordinate. Composing with the bounded Borel drift shows that the integral in (3.3) is defined, measurable from the observation history, and continuous in its upper endpoint. Hence $hat(W)$ is coordinatewise continuous and adapted.
  For $0 lt.eq u < v$ and any bounded $cal(H)_u$-measurable random variable $K$, Lemma 9 gives, separately for each $s in \[ u \, v \]$,
  $ bb(E) \[ K b_i \( s \, Y \( s \) \) \] = bb(E) \[ K X_i \] . $
  Bounded Fubini integrates these numerical identities. Since $B_i \( v \) - B_i \( u \)$ is independent of $X$ and the Brownian histories through $u$, it is independent of $cal(H)_u$. Consequently
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.4)")
   $ bb(E) \[ hat(W)_i \( v \) - hat(W)_i \( u \) divides cal(H)_u \] = 0 . $ <eq:innovation-mean>]
  This argument uses no single exceptional set valid for every real time.
  We identify the joint law using conditional characteristic functions: the error on each small interval is of order $h^(3 \/ 2)$, so the accumulated error vanishes under subdivision. Let $theta = \( theta_i \)_(i in I) in bb(R)^I$ have finite support, and put
  $ L_theta = sum_i \| theta_i \| \, #h(2em) sigma_theta^2 = sum_i theta_i^2 \, #h(2em) h = v - u . $
  The increment
  $ D_(u \, v) = sum_i theta_i \( hat(W)_i \( v \) - hat(W)_i \( u \) \) = Q + R $
  has a conditionally Gaussian term $Q = sum_i theta_i \( B_i \( v \) - B_i \( u \) \)$ of conditional variance $h sigma_theta^2$, and a correction satisfying $\| R \| lt.eq h L_theta$. It follows, without assuming that $Q$ and $R$ are independent, that
  $ lr(|bb(E) \[ D_(u \, v)^2 divides cal(H)_u \] - h sigma_theta^2|) & lt.eq 2 L_theta sigma_theta h^(3 \/ 2) + L_theta^2 h^2 \,\
bb(E) \[ \| D_(u \, v) \|^3 divides cal(H)_u \] & lt.eq 4 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] c_3 sigma_theta^3 h^(3 \/ 2) + L_theta^3 h^3 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, $
  where $c_3 = bb(E) \| N \( 0 \, 1 \) \|^3$. Together with (3.4), the Taylor bound $\| e^(upright(i) x) - 1 - upright(i) x + x^2 \/ 2 \| lt.eq \| x \|^3 \/ 6$ yields a deterministic constant $C_theta$ such that, for $h lt.eq 1$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.5)")
   $ lr(|bb(E) \[ e^(upright(i) D_(u \, v)) divides cal(H)_u \] - e^(- h sigma_theta^2 \/ 2)|) lt.eq C_theta h^(3 \/ 2) . $ <eq:innovation-characteristic>]
  Fix $s < t$ and $A in cal(H)_s$. Partition $\[ s \, t \]$ into $N$ equal intervals with endpoints $t_0 = s \, dots.h \, t_N = t$ and length $h lt.eq 1$. Set
  $ a = e^(- h sigma_theta^2 \/ 2) \, #h(2em) mu_k = bb(E) #h(-0.16667em) [upright(bold(1))_A product_(r = 1)^k e^(upright(i) D_(t_(r - 1) \, t_r))] . $
  The preceding factors are $cal(H)_(t_(k - 1))$-measurable and have modulus one. Thus (3.5) gives $mu_k = a mu_(k - 1) + delta_k$ with $\| delta_k \| lt.eq C_theta h^(3 \/ 2)$. Iterating, and using $0 < a lt.eq 1$, gives
  $ lr(|bb(E) \[ upright(bold(1))_A e^(upright(i) D_(s \, t)) \] - bb(P) \( A \) e^(- \( t - s \) sigma_theta^2 \/ 2)|) lt.eq N C_theta h^(3 \/ 2) arrow.r 0 . $
  For every finite set of coordinates and every $A in cal(H)_s$, uniqueness of finite-dimensional characteristic functions identifies the increment law restricted to $A$ as $bb(P) \( A \)$ times the centered Gaussian law with covariance $\( t - s \) "Id"$. This proves the vector increment law and independence from the entire observation past. Repeating over successive time intervals gives all joint finite-dimensional Brownian laws. Continuous paths, starting at zero, and rational-time evaluations determine the law on the countable product of continuous-path spaces. This proves the claim. ◻
]

== 4 A deterministic inverse and a Brownian sampler <sec:decoder>

The response estimate permits us to reconstruct the observations from their innovations. We first prove the deterministic statement on the full product path space. For a countable set $I$, write

$ cal(C)_I = product_(i in I) C \( \[ 0 \, oo \) \, bb(R) \) \, $

with the product of the compact-open topologies and its Borel sigma-field. For $z in cal(C)_I$, let $z \( t \) = \( z_i \( t \) \)_(i in I)$.

#thm([Lemma 4.1.])[
  (Deterministic inversion).  #emph[Let $b : \[ 0 \, oo \) times bb(R)^I arrow.r \[ 0 \, 1 \]^I$ be jointly Borel. Suppose that a constant $0 lt.eq L < oo$ satisfies 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.1)")
 $ sup_(i in I) \| b_i \( t \, y \) - b_i \( t \, y' \) \| lt.eq L sup_(j in I) \| y_j - y'_j \| $ <eq:abstract-lipschitz>]

 for every $t gt.eq 0$ and every $y \, y' in bb(R)^I$ whose coordinate differences have finite supremum. Then, for every $w in cal(C)_I$, there is a unique $Z \( w \) in cal(C)_I$ satisfying 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.2)")
 $ Z_i \( w \) \( t \) = w_i \( t \) + integral_0^t b_i \( s \, Z \( w \) \( s \) \) thin d s \, #h(2em) i in I \, quad t gt.eq 0 . $ <eq:inverse-equation>]

 The map $Z : cal(C)_I arrow.r cal(C)_I$ is Borel and causal: its restriction to $\[ 0 \, T \]$ depends only on the input restricted to $\[ 0 \, T \]$. It commutes with every coordinate relabeling under which the drift is equivariant.]
] <lem:inverse>

#proof[
  All integrals below are ordinary scalar Lebesgue integrals. Define
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.3)")
   $ Z^0 \( w \) = w \, #h(2em) Z_i^(k + 1) \( w \) \( t \) = w_i \( t \) + integral_0^t b_i \( s \, Z^k \( w \) \( s \) \) thin d s \, #h(2em) k gt.eq 0 . $ <eq:picard>]
  These iterates are defined for every input and have continuous coordinates. Indeed, a continuous coordinate path family gives a Borel map into $bb(R)^I$, so the integrand is Borel and bounded; its indefinite integral is continuous. The initial increment is bounded by $t$, uniformly in $i$. Induction using (4.1) gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.4)")
   $ sup_(i in I) \| Z_i^(k + 1) \( w \) \( t \) - Z_i^k \( w \) \( t \) \| lt.eq frac(L^k t^(k + 1), \( k + 1 \) !) \, #h(2em) k gt.eq 0 \, $ <eq:picard-bound>]
  where $L^0 = 1$, including when $L = 0$. At the induction step the differences of the two input fields are bounded by the preceding estimate, so the hypothesis of (4.1) applies.
  For every finite $T$, the bounds in (4.4), with $t$ replaced by $T$, have finite sum. Thus the iterates converge to a limit $Z \( w \)$ uniformly over all coordinates and all $t in \[ 0 \, T \]$ in the sense that
  $ sup_(i in I) sup_(0 lt.eq t lt.eq T) \| Z_i \( w \) \( t \) - Z_i^k \( w \) \( t \) \| arrow.r 0 . $
  Each coordinate of the limit is continuous. The Lipschitz estimate then gives uniform convergence of the corresponding drifts on $\[ 0 \, T \]$, allowing passage through each integral in (4.3). This proves (4.2). The uniform estimates concern differences from the same input: the input itself may be unbounded over $I$, and its coordinates may have no common modulus of continuity.
  For any other solution $z$, boundedness of $b$ gives $sup_i \| z_i \( t \) - w_i \( t \) \| lt.eq t$. Repeating the same induction yields
  $ sup_(i in I) \| z_i \( t \) - Z_i^k \( w \) \( t \) \| lt.eq frac(L^k t^(k + 1), \( k + 1 \) !) . $
  Letting $k arrow.r oo$ proves uniqueness for every input.
  We next verify measurability on this entire path space. The evaluation map $\( s \, z \) mapsto z \( s \)$ from $\[ 0 \, oo \) times cal(C)_I$ to $bb(R)^I$ is continuous. If $Z^k$ is Borel, the integrand $\( s \, w \) mapsto b_i \( s \, Z^k \( w \) \( s \) \)$ is jointly Borel. Parameterized integration of a bounded Borel function shows that its integral from $0$ to $t$ is Borel in $\( t \, w \)$. Consequently every rational-time coordinate evaluation of $Z^(k + 1)$ is Borel. Such evaluations generate the Borel sigma-field of $cal(C)_I$, proving inductively that every $Z^k$ is Borel. Their limits at rational times prove that $Z$ is Borel as well.
  Finally, the iteration uses only times up to its time argument, which proves causality. A coordinate relabeling that intertwines the drifts also intertwines each step of (4.3) and hence its limit. This applies equally to a bijection between two different countable index sets with corresponding drifts. ◻
]

Here is the sampling consequence in a form that isolates the role of the response estimate.

#thm([Proposition 4.2.])[
  (Sampling from independent Brownian paths).  #emph[Let $nu$ be a probability law on ${ 0 \, 1 }^I$, where $I$ is countable, and for each $i$ choose finite sets $S_n \( i \)$ increasing to $I$. Interpret the finite-tilt functions (2.7) and the limsup drift (2.9) with this law and these sets. Suppose that the finite-tilt functions $b_(i \, S_n \( i \)) \( t \, dot.op \)$ are $L$-Lipschitz in the supremum norm on their finite fields, with one constant $0 lt.eq L < oo$ for all $i \, n$ and $t gt.eq 0$. Then the drift has the Borel solution map of Lemma 11, and the Borel map $Psi : cal(C)_I arrow.r { 0 \, 1 }^I$ defined by 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.5)")
 $ Psi_i \( w \) = upright(bold(1)) {limsup_(n arrow.r oo) frac(Z_i \( w \) \( n \), n) > 1 / 2} $ <eq:readout>]

 sends the law of independent standard Brownian paths to $nu$. The map $Psi$ inherits every coordinate relabeling equivariance of the drift.]
] <prop:brownian-sampler>

#proof[
  Each finite tilt is a ratio of finite sums with a strictly positive denominator, and is therefore jointly Borel in time and the field. Its limsup is jointly Borel and takes values in $\[ 0 \, 1 \]$. If $d = sup_j \| y_j - y'_j \| < oo$, the assumed response bound gives
  $ \| b_(i \, S_n \( i \)) \( t \, y \) - b_(i \, S_n \( i \)) \( t \, y' \) \| lt.eq L d $
  for every $i \, n \, t$. Taking limsups in both directions proves (4.1). Lemma 11 therefore applies. Formula (4.5) is a Borel predicate on every input, including those on which the displayed slopes do not converge.
  To identify its full output law, use the reference observation model: take $X tilde.op nu$, independently take standard Brownian paths $\( B_i \)$, and put $Y_i \( t \) = t X_i + B_i \( t \)$. Lemma 9 identifies the drift with the posterior mean at each fixed time, and Lemma 10 shows that
  $ hat(W)_i \( t \) = Y_i \( t \) - integral_0^t b_i \( s \, Y \( s \) \) thin d s $
  has the law of independent standard Brownian paths. By this definition, the identity
  $ Y_i \( t \) = hat(W)_i \( t \) + integral_0^t b_i \( s \, Y \( s \) \) thin d s $
  holds for all $i$ and all $t$ on every realization of the continuous paths. It does not require the fixed-time posterior identities to hold on one event simultaneously at every time. Deterministic uniqueness in Lemma 11 consequently gives $Y = Z \( hat(W) \)$ as path families. Since $Z$ is Borel, it follows that independent Brownian input $w$ produces the complete joint path law of $Y$ under $Z$.
  For each $i in I$ and $delta > 0$, the Gaussian tail bound gives
  $ bb(P) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \| B_i \( n \) \| > delta n #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] lt.eq 2 exp \( - delta^2 n \/ 2 \) \, #h(2em) n gt.eq 1 . $
  These probabilities are summable. Borel–Cantelli and a countable intersection over $i$ and positive rational $delta$ give $B_i \( n \) \/ n arrow.r 0$ for every $i$ simultaneously, almost surely. Hence $Y_i \( n \) \/ n arrow.r X_i$ simultaneously, and $Psi \( hat(W) \) = X$ almost surely. This identifies the entire configuration law of $Psi \( w \)$ as $nu$. Equivariance follows from Lemma 11 and the coordinatewise formula (4.5). ◻
]

